  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nRain showers overperformed this past evening and early this\nmorning in a zone of broad warm air advection and isentropic \nascent along and north of a warm front. This band of light rain \nis gradually lifting north with the frontal boundary, but \nincreased POPS along the lakeshore of northern Ohio and NW PA \nthrough 14Z this morning to reflect a slower exit. Additional \nQPF will only be a trace to few hundredths of an inch. \n\nBy mid morning, any remaining showers will exit NW PA as the\narea becomes firmly in the warm sector. A tightening pressure\ngradient between surface high pressure over the Mid Atlantic\nregion and a cold front approaching from the western Great Lakes\nwill lead to gusty SSW winds over 20 knots at times, and this\ncombined with 850 mb temps rising to 17-18 C will boost highs\ninto the low 80s this afternoon. The aforementioned cold front\nis associated with a deepening mid/upper trough over the\nnorthern Plains and northern/central Great Lakes, leading to\nstrong height falls over the southern Great Lakes and Ohio\nValley by tonight. This trough still looks to absorb the old\ncut off low that impacted the Desert Southwest earlier in the\nweek, but the jet support and resultant surface wave on the\nfrontal boundary is trending weaker. This will result in more of\na frontal passage rain event tonight as opposed to a surface\nwave riding along the boundary, so QPF is trending lighter.\nNevertheless, the front will be slow to cross the region given\nthe strength of the ridge at the surface and aloft over the Mid\nAtlantic and SE CONUS, so expect the band of rain showers to\nlast from tonight into Friday afternoon, only slowly ending from\nNW to SE. The right entrance of a modest 80+ knot H3 jet\nrounding the base of the trough also supports enough\ndynamics/frontogenesis for widespread rain showers tonight into\nFriday, so slightly increased POPS from 23Z this evening through\n18Z Friday as the rain slowly sinks NW to SE. Minor instability\nthis evening could cause isolated thunder, but this will\nlargely be stratiform rain. Overall, looking at 0.40 to 0.80\ninches of QPF across the region, with the highest amounts in NW\nOhio and near the lakeshore.\n\nDrier air will quickly work into the region Friday evening and \nFriday night, but it will be the start of a much cooler pattern\nas the elongated mid/upper trough takes up residence from the\nnorthern Plains through the Great Lakes and Northeast CONUS, and\na strong Canadian high at the surface builds down across the\nGreat Lakes. This will lead to a chilly night Friday night, with\nlows dipping into the low/mid 40s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe mid/upper trough and Canadian high will remain across the\nGreat Lakes Saturday before another reinforcing shortwave and\nassociated surface low tracking near Hudson Bay brings another\ncold front through the region Sunday, effectively reloading the\ntrough and allowing an even more impressive 1025-1030 mb surface\nhigh to build down from the Upper Midwest early next week. This\nwill keep cool and dry conditions in place, with temperatures\neventually averaging 10 degrees below normal Monday and Tuesday.\nExpect highs in the mid 60s Saturday to slightly rebound into\nthe upper 60s to near 70 Sunday before dropping into the upper\n50s/low 60s Monday and Tuesday. Lows in the mid 30s to low 40s\nMonday night and Tuesday night could support the first frost of\nthe season in portions of interior north central and NE Ohio and\nNW PA. Gradual moderation is expected by Wednesday.",
